---
import Layout from '../../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Upwork Proposal Kit | Redefining Retirement"
  description="A simple way to dip your toe into freelancing and consulting after 50. A cover letter you can actually use, the messages I send once a client replies, a way to look for adjacent work, and the prompt I'd paste into an agent today."
  type="article"
  noindex={true}
  bare={true}
>
  <article class="section">
    <div class="container narrow">

      <header class="article-header">
        <p class="issue-stamp">The Upwork Proposal Kit · Subscriber Edition</p>
        <h1 class="article-title">Upwork Proposal Kit</h1>
        <p class="article-deck">A simple way to dip your toe into freelancing and consulting after 50. A cover letter you can actually use, the messages I send once a client replies, a way to look for adjacent work, and the prompt I'd paste into an agent today.</p>
        <p class="byline">By Corey Koehler · Redefining Retirement · Class of '90</p>
      </header>

      <div class="hello-section">
        <img
          src="/images/corey-portrait.jpg"
          alt="Corey Koehler"
          class="hello-photo"
          onerror="this.style.display='none'"
        />
        <div class="hello-text">
          <h2>Hello</h2>
          <p>Hey, I'm Corey Koehler.</p>
          <p>Foundry worker, then 17 years behind a CAD station as a mechanical draftsman, then midlife solopreneur. I have been writing Upwork proposals, off and on, since the site was still called Elance.</p>
          <p>This kit is the first step I would hand you if you wanted to test freelancing or consulting after 50. It is not a promise that Upwork will work for you, or that an AI agent will land you clients. It is a letter, a prompt, and a plain way to start the conversation.</p>
          <p>Let's get into it.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <nav class="contents" aria-label="Contents">
        <p class="result-section-label">Contents</p>
        <ol>
          <li><a href="#start-here">Start here</a></li>
          <li><a href="#the-2026-conversational-cover-letter">The 2026 conversational cover letter</a></li>
          <li><a href="#how-i-handle-a-potential-client">How I handle a potential client</a></li>
          <li><a href="#look-for-adjacent-work">Look for adjacent work</a></li>
          <li><a href="#dont-shy-away-from-teach-me-jobs">Don't shy away from teach-me jobs</a></li>
          <li><a href="#what-should-i-charge">What should I charge?</a></li>
          <li><a href="#what-service-should-i-offer">What service should I offer?</a></li>
          <li><a href="#the-ai-proposal-prompt">The AI proposal prompt</a></li>
          <li><a href="#how-id-use-an-agent-with-the-official-upwork-connector">How I'd use an agent with the official Upwork connector</a></li>
          <li><a href="#what-top-upwork-educators-say-plus-my-own-tactics">What top Upwork educators say, plus my own tactics</a></li>
        </ol>
      </nav>

      <section class="kit-section" id="start-here">
        <h2>Start here</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>Freelancing and consulting are a good business to just start after 50.</p>
          <p>You already have skills someone will pay for, or you can learn a narrow one without building a company around it first. You can begin in small pieces, on your own schedule, and find out whether you even like the work before you rearrange the rest of your life.</p>
          <p>That is the whole point of dipping a toe in. You are not launching a store. You are not hiring a staff. You are testing a way to get paid for what you know how to do.</p>
          <p>Upwork is a simple place to run that test. The jobs are already posted. The payments stay on the platform. You can send a short proposal, have a conversation, and see if there is a fit.</p>
          <p>This kit is the first step I would hand you. It is not a promise that Upwork will work for you, or that an AI agent will land you clients. It is a cover letter you can actually use, a prompt you can paste into Grok Bot or another agent, and a plain explanation of how I'd use Upwork's new official connector if I were setting this up today.</p>
          <p>Here is what I would do first.</p>
        </div>
        <ol class="step-list">
          <li>Write down two or three kinds of work you can already do well. Keep it plain. "I can set up a simple newsletter." "I can run Google Ads for a small shop." "I can clean up a messy WordPress site." Then list the tools and platforms around those skills, not just the skill name. There is a simple method for that later in this kit.</li>
          <li>Make a short Knowledge Section for yourself: those skills, a few years or examples you can stand behind, and any testimonial you have permission to use. If you are new, say you are new, and point to the experience you do have.</li>
          <li>Use the 2026 cover letter template in this kit. Keep it to about 200 to 300 words. Lead with the client's words, not your bio.</li>
          <li>If you want help with the first draft, paste the AI prompt into Grok Bot or another agent, then paste the job post, then edit before you send anything.</li>
          <li>If you connect Upwork's official agent connector, let it help you search and draft. You still confirm every proposal. Connects are only spent when you say yes.</li>
          <li>When a client replies, stay in Upwork Messages until there is a contract. Use the client-handling notes later in this kit as a starting point, then rewrite them so they sound like you.</li>
        </ol>
        <aside class="pro-tip">
          <p class="pro-tip-label">
            <svg class="bulb" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path fill="currentColor" d="M9 21c0 .55.45 1 1 1h4c.55 0 1-.45 1-1v-1H9v1zm3-19C8.14 2 5 5.14 5 9c0 2.38 1.19 4.47 3 5.74V17c0 .55.45 1 1 1h6c.55 0 1-.45 1-1v-2.26c1.81-1.27 3-3.36 3-5.74 0-3.86-3.14-7-7-7z"/></svg>
            Pro tip
          </p>
          <p>You do not need a new website, a logo, or a six-month plan to try it.</p>
        </aside>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>If you want the slower, fuller picture of starting on Upwork after 50, including whether it is even a fit, I wrote that here: <a href="https://redefiningretirement.io/p/start-a-business-at-50-freelancing-on-upwork">Freelance on Upwork After 50</a>.</p>
          <p>If you are stuck on what to charge, or what service to offer, I already wrote those up: <a href="https://redefiningretirement.io/p/gen-xers-worth-freelancer-upwork">how Gen Xers can find their worth on Upwork</a>, and <a href="https://redefiningretirement.io/p/unique-way-get-ideas-start-business-50-using-google">how to use Google and free tools to find in-demand services at 50</a>. The short versions, plus the videos, are later in this kit.</p>
          <p>This kit is the proposal part, and a simple way to begin.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="kit-section" id="the-2026-conversational-cover-letter">
        <h2>The 2026 conversational cover letter</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>I have been rewriting this letter, off and on, since 2013, when the site was still called Elance. The old version was longer. It also invited people to book a Zoom on my calendar.</p>
          <p>That last part does not belong in a proposal anymore. Before a contract starts, Upwork wants the conversation to stay on the platform. The idea is the same: talk to the client soon. The channel is now Upwork Messages.</p>
          <p>A few other things changed too. Most letters should land around 200 to 300 words. A proposal costs Connects. And I no longer start with "Hello from Minnesota." A simple "Hello," or "Hello [Name]," if you can find the name, does the job.</p>
        </div>
        <aside class="pro-tip">
          <p class="pro-tip-label">
            <svg class="bulb" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path fill="currentColor" d="M9 21c0 .55.45 1 1 1h4c.55 0 1-.45 1-1v-1H9v1zm3-19C8.14 2 5 5.14 5 9c0 2.38 1.19 4.47 3 5.74V17c0 .55.45 1 1 1h6c.55 0 1-.45 1-1v-2.26c1.81-1.27 3-3.36 3-5.74 0-3.86-3.14-7-7-7z"/></svg>
            Pro tip
          </p>
          <p>Clients only see the first two lines of your letter in the results list, along with your name, photo, badges, rate, and Job Success Score.</p>
        </aside>

        <h3 class="block-title">The template</h3>
        <div class="tabs" data-tabs>
          <div class="tablist" role="tablist" aria-label="Cover letter template">
            <button type="button" role="tab" id="letter-tab-1" aria-controls="letter-panel-1" aria-selected="true" tabindex="0">The letter</button>
            <button type="button" role="tab" id="letter-tab-2" aria-controls="letter-panel-2" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">Optional P.S.</button>
            <button type="button" role="tab" id="letter-tab-3" aria-controls="letter-panel-3" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">Past clients</button>
          </div>
          <div role="tabpanel" id="letter-panel-1" aria-labelledby="letter-tab-1" class="is-active" tabindex="0">
            <h3 class="tab-heading">The letter</h3>
            <div class="copy-block">
              <div class="ap-head">
                <p class="prompt-label">The template</p>
                <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-copy>Copy</button>
              </div>
              <pre>Hello [Name],

I can help you [the task, using the client's exact words].

You mentioned [one specific detail from the post]. I have done similar work, including [one relevant result I can actually stand behind], and I would start by [a simple first step].

If you would be open to a short conversation this week, we can do it through Upwork Messages. A video call in the thread works too.

I guarantee you will get useful, specific suggestions from that conversation. If you do not, I will donate $20 to the charity of your choice.

[Your name]

From past clients (optional, only with permission, and only if they match this job):

"[Short quote.]" - [First name, or "a recent client"]</pre>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div role="tabpanel" id="letter-panel-2" aria-labelledby="letter-tab-2" tabindex="0">
            <h3 class="tab-heading">Optional P.S.</h3>
            <div class="prose">
              <p>If you are new to this category on Upwork, and it is actually true, you may add this P.S.:</p>
            </div>
            <div class="copy-block">
              <div class="ap-head">
                <p class="prompt-label">Only if it is true</p>
                <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-copy>Copy</button>
              </div>
              <pre>P.S. I'm fairly new to this category on Upwork and I'm willing to do the first milestone at a 50% discount off my regular rate. Any later work is at my regular rate if we continue.</pre>
            </div>
            <aside class="note">
              <p class="note-label">Note</p>
              <p>Do not use that P.S. as a habit on every bid, and do not offer it if it is not true.</p>
            </aside>
          </div>
          <div role="tabpanel" id="letter-panel-3" aria-labelledby="letter-tab-3" tabindex="0">
            <h3 class="tab-heading">From past clients</h3>
            <div class="prose">
              <p>From past clients (optional, only with permission, and only if they match this job):</p>
            </div>
            <div class="copy-block">
              <div class="ap-head">
                <p class="prompt-label">Only with permission, and only if they match this job</p>
                <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-copy>Copy</button>
              </div>
              <pre>"[Short quote.]" - [First name, or "a recent client"]</pre>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h3 class="block-title">How to fill it in</h3>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>Line 1 is "Hello [Name]," if the name is in the post or in a review you can actually see. Otherwise use "Hello,".</p>
        </div>
        <aside class="pro-tip">
          <p class="pro-tip-label">
            <svg class="bulb" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path fill="currentColor" d="M9 21c0 .55.45 1 1 1h4c.55 0 1-.45 1-1v-1H9v1zm3-19C8.14 2 5 5.14 5 9c0 2.38 1.19 4.47 3 5.74V17c0 .55.45 1 1 1h6c.55 0 1-.45 1-1v-2.26c1.81-1.27 3-3.36 3-5.74 0-3.86-3.14-7-7-7z"/></svg>
            Pro tip
          </p>
          <p>Do not invent a name.</p>
        </aside>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>Line 2 has to work as a preview. Use the client's words for the work they want done. "I can help you redesign the checkout page so fewer people abandon their cart" is better than "I am a highly experienced designer."</p>
          <p>The next paragraph is proof you read the post. One observation, one relevant result, one simple first step. If you do not have a matching result, do not invent one. Show that you understood the job, and be honest about what you have done.</p>
          <p>Invite a conversation in Upwork Messages. Do not add a Calendly link, a personal Zoom link, an email, a phone number, a LinkedIn profile, or a website with a contact form.</p>
          <p>The $20 charity guarantee is on by default in my own prompt, because I still use it. You can delete that line if it does not sound like you.</p>
          <p>Sign with your name only.</p>
        </div>
        <aside class="note">
          <p class="note-label">Note</p>
          <p>Answer every screening question in the screening boxes. Attach a sample that matches this job, if you have one. If a job asks for an Uma video interview instead of a cover letter, do not force this template into that slot.</p>
        </aside>

        <h3 class="block-title">After they say yes</h3>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>Once the client messages you, the work shifts to the conversation. Stay in Upwork Messages until a contract is in place. Do not send a Calendly link, a personal Zoom link, an email, a phone number, or a LinkedIn profile.</p>
          <p>The messages below are the kind I actually send, or would send, in that thread. They are not meant to be pasted word for word. Change the wording so it sounds like you.</p>
        </div>
        <aside class="pro-tip">
          <p class="pro-tip-label">
            <svg class="bulb" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path fill="currentColor" d="M9 21c0 .55.45 1 1 1h4c.55 0 1-.45 1-1v-1H9v1zm3-19C8.14 2 5 5.14 5 9c0 2.38 1.19 4.47 3 5.74V17c0 .55.45 1 1 1h6c.55 0 1-.45 1-1v-2.26c1.81-1.27 3-3.36 3-5.74 0-3.86-3.14-7-7-7z"/></svg>
            Pro tip
          </p>
          <p>A canned reply is easy to spot.</p>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <section class="kit-section" id="how-i-handle-a-potential-client">
        <h2>How I handle a potential client</h2>
        <div class="tabs" data-tabs>
          <div class="tablist" role="tablist" aria-label="Client scripts">
            <button type="button" role="tab" id="script-tab-1" aria-controls="script-panel-1" aria-selected="true" tabindex="0">They reply</button>
            <button type="button" role="tab" id="script-tab-2" aria-controls="script-panel-2" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">Discovery call</button>
            <button type="button" role="tab" id="script-tab-3" aria-controls="script-panel-3" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">Scoping</button>
            <button type="button" role="tab" id="script-tab-4" aria-controls="script-panel-4" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">The quote</button>
            <button type="button" role="tab" id="script-tab-5" aria-controls="script-panel-5" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">A polite no</button>
            <button type="button" role="tab" id="script-tab-6" aria-controls="script-panel-6" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">Follow-up</button>
          </div>

          <div role="tabpanel" id="script-panel-1" aria-labelledby="script-tab-1" class="is-active" tabindex="0">
            <h3 class="tab-heading">Reply when they show interest</h3>
            <div class="copy-block">
              <div class="ap-head">
                <p class="prompt-label">Change the wording so it sounds like you</p>
                <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-copy>Copy</button>
              </div>
              <pre>Hello [first name],

Thanks for getting back to me. I'm glad this looks like it could be a fit.

I have a few questions so I can scope this properly, and I'm happy to hop on a short call right here in Upwork Messages if that's easier. The phone icon in this thread starts a Zoom or a voice call, so we can keep everything on the platform.

What times work for you this week?

[Your name]</pre>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div role="tabpanel" id="script-panel-2" aria-labelledby="script-tab-2" tabindex="0">
            <h3 class="tab-heading">Invite a discovery call inside Upwork</h3>
            <div class="copy-block">
              <div class="ap-head">
                <p class="prompt-label">Change the wording so it sounds like you</p>
                <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-copy>Copy</button>
              </div>
              <pre>Hello [first name],

Thanks for being open to a conversation.

We can do a short Zoom or voice call right in Upwork Messages (the phone icon in the thread). Tell me a couple of times that work for you this week, and I will send the in-Upwork call when we pick one.

Looking forward to talking.

[Your name]</pre>
            </div>
            <aside class="note">
              <p class="note-label">Note</p>
              <p>After a contract is in place, you can use your own calendar or Zoom if you want. Not before.</p>
            </aside>
          </div>

          <div role="tabpanel" id="script-panel-3" aria-labelledby="script-tab-3" tabindex="0">
            <h3 class="tab-heading">Scoping questions</h3>
            <div class="prose">
              <p>If they would rather stay in chat, or you want a little context before the call, keep the questions short and specific.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="copy-block">
              <div class="ap-head">
                <p class="prompt-label">Ask only what you actually need</p>
                <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-copy>Copy</button>
              </div>
              <pre>Before we jump on a call, or if you'd rather handle this in messages, here's what would help me quote this accurately:

1. What does done look like for you?
2. Is there a deadline I should know about?
3. What have you already tried, if anything?
4. Who will I be working with, and how should we check in?
5. Is this a one-time project, or are you hoping this turns into ongoing help?

No need to write a novel. A few sentences on each is plenty.</pre>
            </div>
            <aside class="note">
              <p class="note-label">Note</p>
              <p>Ask only what you actually need. If the job post already answered one of these, don't ask it again just to have a list.</p>
            </aside>
          </div>

          <div role="tabpanel" id="script-panel-4" aria-labelledby="script-tab-4" tabindex="0">
            <h3 class="tab-heading">A quote or next-step message</h3>
            <div class="prose">
              <p>Send this after you understand the work, not before.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="copy-block">
              <div class="ap-head">
                <p class="prompt-label">Change the wording so it sounds like you</p>
                <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-copy>Copy</button>
              </div>
              <pre>Thanks for the conversation, [first name].

Based on what you described, I would approach it like this: [two or three plain steps].

For this scope, my quote is [amount] for [what's included], with [a realistic timeline]. If you want to start, I can send a contract through Upwork with a first milestone of [the first piece of work].

If anything in the scope should change, tell me and I'll adjust the quote before we start.

[Your name]</pre>
            </div>
            <aside class="note">
              <p class="note-label">Note</p>
              <p>Put the official offer or contract through Upwork. Don't move payment off the platform.</p>
            </aside>
          </div>

          <div role="tabpanel" id="script-panel-5" aria-labelledby="script-tab-5" tabindex="0">
            <h3 class="tab-heading">A polite decline</h3>
            <div class="prose">
              <p>Say no when you are not the right fit. That is better than stretching, or going quiet.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="copy-block">
              <div class="ap-head">
                <p class="prompt-label">Change the wording so it sounds like you</p>
                <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-copy>Copy</button>
              </div>
              <pre>Thanks for thinking of me for this, [first name].

After reading it through, I don't think I'm the right person for [the honest reason: the timeline, the type of work, or that this is not work I do]. I would rather tell you that now than waste your time.

I hope you find the right person quickly.

[Your name]</pre>
            </div>
            <aside class="note">
              <p class="note-label">Note</p>
              <p>You do not owe them a referral. If you genuinely know someone who is a better fit, and you are comfortable saying so, you can mention it. Don't invent one.</p>
            </aside>
          </div>

          <div role="tabpanel" id="script-panel-6" aria-labelledby="script-tab-6" tabindex="0">
            <h3 class="tab-heading">A follow-up</h3>
            <div class="prose">
              <p>Give it a few days, not a few hours. If they already said they need time, wait longer than that.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="copy-block">
              <div class="ap-head">
                <p class="prompt-label">One follow-up is usually enough</p>
                <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-copy>Copy</button>
              </div>
              <pre>Hello [first name],

Just floating this back up in case it got buried. I'm still available to help with [the project], and I'm happy to answer questions here in this thread.

If the timing isn't right, no worries at all.

[Your name]</pre>
            </div>
            <aside class="note">
              <p class="note-label">Note</p>
              <p>One follow-up is usually enough. If they don't reply after that, leave it alone and go write the next proposal.</p>
            </aside>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="kit-section" id="look-for-adjacent-work">
        <h2>Look for adjacent work</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>Don't only search your exact skill.</p>
          <p>Search the platforms and tools around it too.</p>
        </div>
        <aside class="pro-tip">
          <p class="pro-tip-label">
            <svg class="bulb" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path fill="currentColor" d="M9 21c0 .55.45 1 1 1h4c.55 0 1-.45 1-1v-1H9v1zm3-19C8.14 2 5 5.14 5 9c0 2.38 1.19 4.47 3 5.74V17c0 .55.45 1 1 1h6c.55 0 1-.45 1-1v-2.26c1.81-1.27 3-3.36 3-5.74 0-3.86-3.14-7-7-7z"/></svg>
            Pro tip
          </p>
          <p>The job you can actually do is often posted under the software name, not under the job title you used at work.</p>
        </aside>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>A few examples from how I think about this:</p>
        </div>
        <div class="tabs" data-tabs>
          <div class="tablist" role="tablist" aria-label="Adjacent work examples">
            <button type="button" role="tab" id="adj-tab-1" aria-controls="adj-panel-1" aria-selected="true" tabindex="0">Newsletters</button>
            <button type="button" role="tab" id="adj-tab-2" aria-controls="adj-panel-2" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">HR</button>
            <button type="button" role="tab" id="adj-tab-3" aria-controls="adj-panel-3" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">Bookkeeping</button>
            <button type="button" role="tab" id="adj-tab-4" aria-controls="adj-panel-4" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">Drafting</button>
          </div>
          <div role="tabpanel" id="adj-panel-1" aria-labelledby="adj-tab-1" class="is-active" tabindex="0">
            <h3 class="tab-heading">Newsletters</h3>
            <p>If you can help with newsletters, search for newsletter writing or setup. Also search for jobs where someone needs help setting up Mailchimp or Beehiiv.</p>
          </div>
          <div role="tabpanel" id="adj-panel-2" aria-labelledby="adj-tab-2" tabindex="0">
            <h3 class="tab-heading">HR</h3>
            <p>If you come from HR, search for HR work. Also search for help with the platforms HR professionals actually use.</p>
          </div>
          <div role="tabpanel" id="adj-panel-3" aria-labelledby="adj-tab-3" tabindex="0">
            <h3 class="tab-heading">Bookkeeping</h3>
            <p>If you can help with bookkeeping or finance, search for bookkeeper jobs. Also search for jobs asking for QuickBooks help or Xero help.</p>
          </div>
          <div role="tabpanel" id="adj-panel-4" aria-labelledby="adj-tab-4" tabindex="0">
            <h3 class="tab-heading">Drafting</h3>
            <p>In my case, I was a mechanical draftsman. I could look for drafting work. I could also look for people who needed help with SolidWorks or AutoCAD. That adjacent search has mattered for me. Years ago I helped an engineering department move from 2D AutoCAD to 3D SolidWorks, and that kind of experience has helped me win work later.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>The method is simple. Give yourself 20 minutes and make three short lists:</p>
        </div>
        <ol class="trio">
          <li>
            <span class="trio-num">1</span>
            <p>The work you already do. Newsletter setup. Google Ads. Drafting. HR support. Bookkeeping. Keep it in plain language.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="trio-num">2</span>
            <p>The tools and platforms you used to do that work. Mailchimp. Beehiiv. SolidWorks. AutoCAD. QuickBooks. Xero. The HR system you actually know.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="trio-num">3</span>
            <p>The people you used to help. Creators. Small manufacturers. An engineering team. An HR manager. A small-business owner.</p>
          </li>
        </ol>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>Then go to Upwork, choose Jobs, and search each line, one at a time. Write down how many posts you see, and whether you could honestly do that work. Keep the searches that have real jobs you can do. Set aside the ones that are a stretch.</p>
          <p>You are not trying to become a new kind of freelancer overnight. You are trying to notice the jobs that already sit next to the skill you have.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="kit-section" id="dont-shy-away-from-teach-me-jobs">
        <h2>Don't shy away from teach-me jobs</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>A lot of freelancers skip posts where the client doesn't want to hire someone to do the work. They just want someone to teach them how to do it.</p>
          <p>I would not skip those.</p>
          <p>I have had multiple clients ask me to teach them Google Ads at an hourly rate. I helped them that way. Often, once we got into it, they started to see how much work it is, and how much you need to know to do it well. At that point, many of them decided it was better to have me run it. That can turn into a longer project where I just manage it.</p>
        </div>
        <aside class="pro-tip">
          <p class="pro-tip-label">
            <svg class="bulb" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path fill="currentColor" d="M9 21c0 .55.45 1 1 1h4c.55 0 1-.45 1-1v-1H9v1zm3-19C8.14 2 5 5.14 5 9c0 2.38 1.19 4.47 3 5.74V17c0 .55.45 1 1 1h6c.55 0 1-.45 1-1v-2.26c1.81-1.27 3-3.36 3-5.74 0-3.86-3.14-7-7-7z"/></svg>
            Pro tip
          </p>
          <p>That is my experience. It is not a promise that a teaching job will become a managed retainer for you, or that it will pay a certain amount. Some people really do want to learn it and keep doing it themselves. That is a perfectly good outcome too.</p>
        </aside>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>If the post is clearly a teaching request, say so up front. If you are already on a call and it sounds like they are on the fence, you can offer both paths and let them choose.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="tabs" data-tabs>
          <div class="tablist" role="tablist" aria-label="Teach-me scripts">
            <button type="button" role="tab" id="teach-tab-1" aria-controls="teach-panel-1" aria-selected="true" tabindex="0">Teach them</button>
            <button type="button" role="tab" id="teach-tab-2" aria-controls="teach-panel-2" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">Take it over</button>
          </div>
          <div role="tabpanel" id="teach-panel-1" aria-labelledby="teach-tab-1" class="is-active" tabindex="0">
            <h3 class="tab-heading">Pitching a teach-me engagement</h3>
            <div class="copy-block">
              <div class="ap-head">
                <p class="prompt-label">Say so up front if the post is a teaching request</p>
                <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-copy>Copy</button>
              </div>
              <pre>I can also teach you how to do this yourself, at an hourly rate, if that's what you want. We can work through [the task] together on Upwork calls, and I'll show you the parts I would normally just go do.

Some people like having that option. Some decide, after a few sessions, that they'd rather have me run it. Either way is fine with me. We can start with a short teaching session and see how it feels.</pre>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div role="tabpanel" id="teach-panel-2" aria-labelledby="teach-tab-2" tabindex="0">
            <h3 class="tab-heading">Offering to take it over</h3>
            <aside class="note">
              <p class="note-label">Note</p>
              <p>Only send this if it is actually true, after they have seen the work, not as a bait-and-switch in the first proposal.</p>
            </aside>
            <div class="copy-block">
              <div class="ap-head">
                <p class="prompt-label">After they have seen the work</p>
                <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-copy>Copy</button>
              </div>
              <pre>You've got a good handle on the concepts now. The part that usually eats the time is [the ongoing work: the daily checks, the writing, the adjustments, or whatever is actually true].

If you'd rather have me take that over, I'm happy to switch this to a managed project. We can keep the same thread, I'll send a new milestone or a new contract through Upwork, and I'll keep you in the loop.

If you want to keep learning it yourself, we can do that too.</pre>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="kit-section" id="what-should-i-charge">
        <h2>What should I charge?</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>I still use a short Upwork exercise for this, and I wrote it up for Gen Xers who are trying to figure out what their work is worth in the open market, not just what a job used to pay.</p>
          <p>The idea is simple. Spend a few minutes listing the skills, projects, tools, and even hobbies that have actually helped you. Then go to Upwork and search Talent for those skills, to see what other people are charging. Then search Jobs, one skill at a time, and notice how many listings show up. That gives you a fast read on demand, and a reality check on rate, before you invent a number.</p>
          <p>I walked through that in <a href="https://redefiningretirement.io/p/gen-xers-worth-freelancer-upwork">Gen Xers: What Are You Worth As A Freelancer On Upwork?</a>, published Oct 15, 2023. The short companion video, about 8 minutes, shows the Talent and Jobs search.</p>
