              <p class="score-label">replies</p>
              <div class="score-track" aria-hidden="true"><span style="width:66.67%"></span></div>
            </div>
            <div class="score-stat">
              <p class="score-num">4</p>
              <p class="score-label">meetings</p>
              <div class="score-track" aria-hidden="true"><span style="width:33.33%"></span></div>
            </div>
            <div class="score-stat score-stat-result">
              <p class="score-num score-num-money">$1,602.60</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </figure>

        <div class="copy-block">
          <div class="ap-head">
            <p class="prompt-label">The prompt. Paste the job post after it, then edit the draft.</p>
            <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-copy>Copy</button>
          </div>
          <pre>You are my Upwork proposal drafter.

Your job is to draft a short, personalized Upwork cover letter and screening-question answers I can edit and send. You do not send anything. You do not invent jobs, results, tools, or testimonials I did not give you.

Inputs I will provide:
- The job post (paste, including budget, duration, and any screening questions)
- Optional: notes from my research (client profile, past hires, their website)
- My Knowledge Section (skills, years, niches, results, testimonials I have approved for use)
- Optional: "skip the charity-guarantee line" if I do not want it on this job (it is on by default), and whether a first-milestone discount is actually on the table for this job

Hard rules:
1. Stay on Upwork. Do not include a phone number, email address, personal website, LinkedIn or other social handle, Calendly link, personal Zoom link, or any other way to reach me off the platform. Invite a chat or a video call through Upwork Messages only.
2. The first two lines must work as a preview. Line 1: "Hello [Name]," if the client's name is in the post, otherwise "Hello,". Line 2: "I can help you [the task, using the client's exact words]." Do not use a location-based opener.
3. Keep the cover letter to about 200 to 300 words.
4. Use the client's words and the actual requirements. Do not generic-paste.
5. Use only results and testimonials I provided. If I have no matching proof, say so in your notes to me and write the letter without fake numbers.
6. Do not promise income, rankings, or timelines I did not state.
7. Write in a friendly, plain, confident voice. Short paragraphs. No hype, no jargon unless the client used it, and no em dashes.
8. Answer every screening question separately, in a short, direct way.

Letter structure (Conversational Cover Letter):
- The two-line opening above
- One short paragraph that shows I already understand the job (a specific observation or a relevant result)
- One or two brief proof points, only if they match
- A simple approach, only if it helps, in a few lines, not a four-step lecture
- A clear next step: offer a short conversation or video call through Upwork Messages, and ask if they would be open to that this week
- The $20 charity guarantee, on by default unless I said to skip it: "I guarantee you will get useful, specific suggestions from that conversation. If you do not, I will donate $20 to the charity of your choice."
- Sign-off: my name only
- Optional P.S., only if I said a first-milestone discount applies and it is true: "I'm fairly new to this category on Upwork and I'm willing to do the first milestone at a 50% discount off my regular rate. Any later work is at my regular rate if we continue."
- Then, if I provided testimonials, add a short "From past clients" block under the signature, using only the ones that match this job

Also do this, in a short note to me (not in the letter):
- The exact two-line preview
- Which job details you used
- Suggested screening-question answers, labeled
- Boost or not, with a one-line reason (only boost if the fit is strong)
- Anything missing from my Knowledge Section that would have made the letter stronger
- A reminder to edit the draft and review Connects before I send it</pre>
        </div>

        <h3 class="block-title">A simple Knowledge Section you can paste</h3>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>Copy this, then fill it in with only what is true.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="copy-block">
          <div class="ap-head">
            <p class="prompt-label">Leave a line blank if you do not have it</p>
            <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-copy>Copy</button>
          </div>
          <pre>Skills:
Years / background:
Kinds of work I want:
Results I can mention (only real ones):
Testimonials I have permission to use:
I am new to Upwork: yes / no
First-milestone discount is on the table for this job: yes / no
Skip the charity-guarantee line: yes / no</pre>
        </div>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>If the agent asks for more, give it more. If you do not have a number, leave it blank. A short honest brief is better than a padded one.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="kit-section" id="how-id-use-an-agent-with-the-official-upwork-connector">
        <h2>How I'd use an agent with the official Upwork connector</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>This is a plan, not a story about results I have already gotten with this tool.</p>
          <p>I connected Upwork's official connector to Grok Bot for a couple of days. I have not used it to write or send proposals, and I have not used it to land clients. I still think it would have helped me, 10 years ago and 2 or 3 years ago, because it can take some of the daily checking and first-draft work off your plate.</p>
        </div>

        <h3 class="block-title">What it can do</h3>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>Upwork's official MCP, at <a href="https://www.upwork.com/ai/mcp">https://www.upwork.com/ai/mcp</a>, lets an AI agent talk to your Upwork account after you sign in with OAuth. There is no extra fee for the connector. Standard Upwork fees and Connects still apply.</p>
          <p>For freelancers, the official page says the agent can:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Show a dashboard of invitations, offers, messages, matched jobs, and your Connects balance</li>
            <li>Search jobs, pull matches based on your profile, and save favorites</li>
            <li>Draft a proposal from your profile, your past work, and the job, then submit it after you review it</li>
            <li>Help with invitations and offers (accepting an offer still finishes on upwork.com)</li>
            <li>View contracts, submit milestone work, and track time</li>
            <li>Check earnings, and help with some profile and visibility updates</li>
            <li>Read and send messages after you approve them</li>
          </ul>
          <p>You can also ask it to search Upwork's Help Center, and to schedule a video meeting in a message room.</p>
        </div>

        <h3 class="block-title">What it will not do for you</h3>
        <ul class="wont-list">
          <li>It will not submit a proposal, spend Connects, or send a message until you confirm. If a confirmation fails, check your proposals and your Connects balance before you try again.</li>
          <li>Payments and binding offer acceptances finish on upwork.com, not inside the agent.</li>
          <li>It cannot currently create or edit Project Catalog projects.</li>
          <li>It does not score jobs or pick the best ones for you. It can sort and summarize using criteria you give it. You still decide what to pursue.</li>
          <li>A freelancer cannot send the first message on a proposal thread. The client has to message first.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 class="block-title">"Watching jobs," honestly</h3>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>If your agent supports scheduled tasks, it can run a search you set, on a schedule you choose. That is the official, allowed version of watching for jobs.</p>
        </div>
        <aside class="pro-tip">
          <p class="pro-tip-label">
            <svg class="bulb" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path fill="currentColor" d="M9 21c0 .55.45 1 1 1h4c.55 0 1-.45 1-1v-1H9v1zm3-19C8.14 2 5 5.14 5 9c0 2.38 1.19 4.47 3 5.74V17c0 .55.45 1 1 1h6c.55 0 1-.45 1-1v-2.26c1.81-1.27 3-3.36 3-5.74 0-3.86-3.14-7-7-7z"/></svg>
            Pro tip
          </p>
          <p>Do not set it to check every few minutes. Upwork's rate-limit guidance says not to poll that way. If requests start getting refused or slowed, pause the schedule instead of retrying in a loop.</p>
        </aside>

        <h3 class="block-title">Connects, boosting, and rate limits</h3>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>Each Connect is $0.15. The job post shows how many a proposal will use, and that number can change while the post is live.</p>
          <p>Boosting is optional and costs extra Connects. Official help says the cost is shown before you confirm. I would only boost a job that is already a strong fit.</p>
          <p>Upwork also caps how many MCP requests you can make each minute, hour, and day. Those limits can change, and they are listed here: <a href="https://support.upwork.com/hc/en-us/articles/56577997707923-Rate-limits-for-Upwork-MCP">Rate limits for Upwork MCP</a>. One prompt can cause many requests, so be specific when you ask for a search.</p>
        </div>
        <aside class="pro-tip">
          <p class="pro-tip-label">
            <svg class="bulb" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path fill="currentColor" d="M9 21c0 .55.45 1 1 1h4c.55 0 1-.45 1-1v-1H9v1zm3-19C8.14 2 5 5.14 5 9c0 2.38 1.19 4.47 3 5.74V17c0 .55.45 1 1 1h6c.55 0 1-.45 1-1v-2.26c1.81-1.27 3-3.36 3-5.74 0-3.86-3.14-7-7-7z"/></svg>
            Pro tip
          </p>
          <p>Connects are only charged when you confirm.</p>
        </aside>

        <h3 class="block-title">About Grok Bot, and other agents</h3>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>Upwork's official first-party setup guides name Claude, ChatGPT, Cursor, and Codex, plus other MCP-compatible apps that run on your computer and support OAuth. Grok Bot is not on that official list. It is listed in the Grok Bot plugin directory, and I did get it connected for a couple of days. Some hosted agents may still have sign-in limits, because Upwork has to approve the callback URL.</p>
          <p>If the sign-in works for you, the same rules apply. If it does not, stop there. Use a listed official setup, or write the letter from the template in this kit. Do not try to work around a failed login by automating the Upwork website itself.</p>
          <p>The template and the prompt work in other agents too. If you specifically want to try Grok Bot, I put a limited number of invites in the small box below.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="promo invite-box">
          <div class="promo-tag">A limited number of Grok Bot invites</div>
          <h3>Want to try the agent workflow?</h3>
          <p>I have a small number of Grok Bot invites to give away with this kit. Seats are limited, because each referral code can only be redeemed a limited number of times.</p>
          <p>Officially, friends who use the invite get a 2-week free trial of Premium. A credit card is required to start the trial, nothing is charged at checkout, and they send a reminder before the first charge so you can cancel if you do not want to continue.</p>
          <p>Here is the invite: <a href="https://grok.com/refer/bot?code=NNCG9QYS8SDS">https://grok.com/refer/bot?code=NNCG9QYS8SDS</a></p>
          <p>This is a Grok Bot trial invite, not a newsletter referral. You do not have to use Grok Bot to use the rest of this kit.</p>
        </div>

        <h3 class="block-title">A simple weekly rhythm I'd try</h3>
        <ol class="flow" aria-label="Watch jobs, then draft, then you edit and confirm">
          <li>
            <span class="flow-num">1</span>
            <p class="flow-title">Watch jobs</p>
            <p>A search you set, on a schedule you choose. Or check the list yourself, the way I used to.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="flow-num">2</span>
            <p class="flow-title">Draft</p>
            <p>Paste the job into your agent with the prompt and your Knowledge Section. Get a draft.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="flow-num">3</span>
            <p class="flow-title">You edit and confirm</p>
            <p>You still confirm every proposal. Connects are only spent when you say yes.</p>
          </li>
        </ol>
        <ol class="step-list">
          <li>Once a day, or a few times a week, ask the agent for a short list of jobs that match the skills you wrote down. Or check the list yourself, the way I used to.</li>
          <li>Open only the posts that look like a real fit. Read the client. If it is not a fit, skip it.</li>
          <li>Paste the job into your agent with the prompt and your Knowledge Section. Get a draft.</li>
          <li>Rewrite the first two lines yourself. Cut anything you would not say out loud. Check the screening answers.</li>
          <li>Confirm only the proposals you would be proud to send. Review the Connects cost first.</li>
          <li>If a client messages you, keep the conversation in Upwork Messages until a contract starts.</li>
        </ol>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>That is enough to dip a toe in. You can always do more later.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="kit-section" id="what-top-upwork-educators-say-plus-my-own-tactics">
        <h2>What top Upwork educators say, plus my own tactics</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>I am not the only person who has spent years thinking about Upwork proposals. Below are a few well-known educators, paraphrased, with links so you can go read them yourself. Their published income figures stay theirs. Mine stay mine. Where a tip bumps into 2026 Upwork rules, I flag it.</p>
        </div>
        <aside class="note">
          <p class="note-label">Rules check</p>
          <p>The official bar I used as a rules check is Upwork's cover-letter guide, updated May 15, 2026: clients see only the opening lines, plus your name, photo, badges, rate, and Job Success Score; keep most letters to about 200 to 300 words; end with a clear next step. Source: <a href="https://www.upwork.com/resources/cover-letter-tips">Upwork Cover Letter Tips</a>.</p>
        </aside>

        <div class="educators">
          <details class="educator">
            <summary><h3>Lex DeVille</h3></summary>
            <div class="educator-body prose">
              <p>Lex is the educator I already pointed to in my 2022 guide. His courses are still at <a href="https://lexdeville.com/freelance-university">lexdeville.com/freelance-university</a>. A video I cited then is still live: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AxPnHGxdhdw">5 Upwork Bio Tips for Beginners in 2021</a>.</p>
              <p>Worth keeping: make the profile overview about the client's need, not your life story. The first words matter, the same way the first lines of a proposal matter.</p>
              <p>The videos are from 2020 to 2022, so treat them as older. Flag: I once retold his demo of deleting a profile and starting from scratch. Do not copy that. Opening a second account, or treating a reset as a loophole, can conflict with Upwork's account rules. I agree on the client-first overview. This kit does not teach a from-scratch profile stunt.</p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="educator">
            <summary><h3>Josh Burns</h3></summary>
            <div class="educator-body prose">
              <p>Josh's cover-letter walkthrough is <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dOVtO9JVLVY">Upwork Proposal Tutorial for Beginners</a> (Feb 22, 2024), with notes at <a href="https://joshburnstech.com/upwork-proposal-tips/">joshburnstech.com</a>.</p>
              <p>Worth keeping: the first one or two sentences have to earn the click. Use the client's name when you can find it. Attach work that matches this job. Don't paste the same letter every time.</p>
              <p>Flag: asking for a conversation is right. Putting a Calendly link, a personal Zoom link, an email, or a phone number in the proposal is not. Keep the invite in Upwork Messages. I agree on the first two lines and matching samples. I keep the letter to about 200 to 300 words, and I would have an agent draft the first pass for me to edit.</p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="educator">
            <summary><h3>Evan Fisher</h3></summary>
            <div class="educator-body prose">
              <p>Evan's site is <a href="https://freelancemvp.com/">Freelance MVP</a>. A useful public video is <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=f4INGRluDAU">8 Golden Rules for Writing Upwork Cover Letters</a> (Nov 12, 2021).</p>
              <p>Worth keeping: short letter, client's outcome, a little real social proof, one clear next step, a P.S. only if it has a job, and no overpromising.</p>
              <p>The videos I checked stop in 2022, so label them as older. Flag: if "book a call" means Calendly or a personal Zoom before a contract, that conflicts with current rules. Invite the chat in Upwork Messages instead. I agree on short, client-first letters. I am comfortable with a small, useful question in the letter.</p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="educator">
            <summary><h3>Danny Margulies</h3></summary>
            <div class="educator-body prose">
              <p>Danny has a still-live article on Upwork's site: <a href="https://www.upwork.com/resources/leading-voices/write-proposals-freelancer-advice">How to Write Proposals on Upwork: Advice From Freelancers</a>.</p>
              <p>Worth keeping: can you do this specific project, will you make the client's life easier, and do you actually care? Show one relevant example, or make a similar sample, not a free version of their actual job. Treat screening questions as real work. Help. Don't hard-sell.</p>
              <p>Older advice sometimes said the extra questions were the first thing a client saw. Official 2026 guidance now stresses the first two lines of the letter, with screening answers as their own step. Do both. Flag: "don't limit yourself to the client's specified budget" can be honest scoping. It should not become a bait-and-switch. I would now let an agent draft that tailored letter, then edit it.</p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="educator">
            <summary><h3>Melissa Psihudakis</h3></summary>
            <div class="educator-body prose">
              <p>Melissa has two useful pieces on Upwork's Leading Voices: <a href="https://www.upwork.com/resources/leading-voices/write-a-successful-proposal">How To Write a Successful Upwork Proposal</a> (Mar 27, 2024) and <a href="https://www.upwork.com/resources/leading-voices/using-ai-for-job-proposals">5 Ways Freelancers Can Use AI for Better Job Proposals</a> (Nov 20, 2023).</p>
              <p>Worth keeping: show you understood the problem, use AI to jump-start or polish a letter you still edit, wait before a follow-up instead of nudging within hours, and never ask to move the conversation or the payment off Upwork in the proposal.</p>
              <p>The tool names in the 2023 AI piece are dated. The "draft, then edit" rule is not. I agree almost across the board. Where I differ: her structure is a longer, more formal proposal. My 2026 default is a shorter conversational letter and a Messages invite.</p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="educator">
            <summary><h3>Morgan Overholt</h3></summary>
            <div class="educator-body prose">
              <p>Morgan's guide is <a href="https://morganoverholt.com/upwork/upwork-proposal-sample/">Upwork Proposal Sample: 11 Tips</a> (updated Mar 16, 2023), plus <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dxo1Q2qSeuE">a video version</a> and <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YD1ZxCffyZM">6 things not to do</a>.</p>
              <p>Worth keeping: about 200 words, a simple "Hello" if you cannot find a name, proof you read the post, matching samples, and a close that invites a reply on Upwork.</p>
              <p>Useful flag: she used to recommend a regular website link, then updated that after learning a page with a contact form can violate pre-contract rules. A contact-free portfolio page, plus attachments, is the version that fits. Using your real name is fine. Do not add a LinkedIn URL, an email, or a phone number. I would now start from an agent draft, and I keep the $20 charity guarantee as a default confidence line, which she does not use.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>

        <h3 class="block-title">Where I land</h3>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>The overlap is not a coincidence.</p>
          <p>First lines matter. Keep the letter short and about the client. Use real samples. Do not paste a canned letter. Do not take the conversation off Upwork before a contract.</p>
        </div>
        <aside class="pro-tip">
          <p class="pro-tip-label">
            <svg class="bulb" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path fill="currentColor" d="M9 21c0 .55.45 1 1 1h4c.55 0 1-.45 1-1v-1H9v1zm3-19C8.14 2 5 5.14 5 9c0 2.38 1.19 4.47 3 5.74V17c0 .55.45 1 1 1h6c.55 0 1-.45 1-1v-2.26c1.81-1.27 3-3.36 3-5.74 0-3.86-3.14-7-7-7z"/></svg>
            Pro tip
          </p>
          <p>If AI helps, you still edit.</p>
        </aside>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>Where I am more specific: stay inside Upwork Messages, spend Connects on purpose, treat the new official connector as a watcher and a drafter that still needs your yes, and treat any published results I have shared as evidence of the older manual and ChatGPT process, not of this new tool.</p>
          <p>For now, the useful move is smaller than all of this. Pick one kind of work you can already do. Fill in the Knowledge Section. Send a short, honest letter. Edit the draft before you confirm it.</p>
          <p>That is enough to dip your toe in.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="promo">
          <div class="promo-tag">Your Next Move</div>
          <h3>Go deeper on your skills, or fine-tune your agent</h3>
          <p>This kit is the first step: a letter, a search list, and a way to start the conversation. If you want a slower pass at naming what you're good at, and at feeding that into a bot you can actually use, I made a $27 workbook for that. The Gen eXit Compass. It will not land clients for you. It is just a way to get clearer on your skills before you send the next proposal.</p>
          <a href="https://redefiningretirement.io/products/the-gen-exit-compass" class="promo-cta">Get The Gen eXit Compass</a>
        </div>

        <p class="signoff">Have a good one,</p>
        <p class="signoff-name">Corey</p>

        <div class="actions">
          <a href="https://www.redefiningretirement.io" class="btn">Come hang at RedefiningRetirement.io</a>
